    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="1273" n="1241" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p1/pg1241"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.00/4–554: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dillon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>niact</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-04-05T11:00:00+01:00">April 5, 1954—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3711. Limit distribution. In connection with Embassy telegram 3710,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d693-f1"/> Bidault told me that he is more convinced than ever that
                    Soviets very much desire a cease-fire and truce in Indochina because of their
                    deep concern of possible action by Chinese if fighting continues.</p>
                <p>He said that Vinogradov<anchor n="2" xml:id="d693-f2"/> came to see him again and
                    literally pleaded with him to arrange a truce with Ho Chi-minh, saying that
                    fighting in Indochina simply must stop.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d693-f1" n="1" xml:id="d693-f1-content">
                <p>Dated Apr. 5, <ref target="#pg1236">p. 1236</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d693-f2" n="2" xml:id="d693-f2-content">
                <p>Sergey Aleksandrovich Vinogradov, Soviet Ambassador in France.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
